+    "LayoutEditor__Snapping__Config": {
+        "LayoutEditor__Snapping__Description": "Object snaps for placing and dragging dimensions and vectors. Two sources: the projected linework inside 2D viewports, and - with SheetObjects on - the sheet's own vectors and dimensions, so a new line can start on the corner of the last one and a polygon can close exactly on its first point. A dragged vector snaps too: the grab point and every vertex are offered, and the nearest snap moves the whole shape. RadiusPx is the pick radius on screen at any zoom. Endpoints and midpoints are candidates; an endpoint wins a near tie, and the sheet's own markup wins a tie with the linework because it sits on top. The item being dragged never offers its own moving point. HiddenLines adds the hidden class as a source. The toggle (toolbar button or the Snap__Toggle key binding) is remembered per browser. MARKER COLOURS say which tool is snapping: blue for vertices (the Draw and Rectangle tools, vertex grips, and a vector being moved), orange for dimensions (the Dimension tool, its grips and its line inference), purple for viewports (carrying one by a point - the ring, the tracking crosses and the frame outline go purple too). The colours live in Na__LayoutEditor__Styles__Main__.css.",
+        "LayoutEditor__Snapping__Enabled": true,
+        "LayoutEditor__Snapping__RadiusPx": 14,
+        "LayoutEditor__Snapping__Endpoints": true,
+        "LayoutEditor__Snapping__Midpoints": true,
+        "LayoutEditor__Snapping__HiddenLines": false,
+        "LayoutEditor__Snapping__SheetObjects": true,
+        "LayoutEditor__Snapping__SheetChrome": true,
+        "LayoutEditor__Snapping__SheetChromeNote": "The sheet's own paper as snap points (Adam, 21-Sep-2026: 'make points on the title block inferencible and snappable', to start construction lines from): the border's four corners and the middle of each side, the title block strip's corners, every cell divider's two ends and middle, and the notes margin's divider where it meets the border and the title block. A line drawn from the corner of the title block starts exactly there. Endpoints and Midpoints above still decide which kinds are offered; the classic scanned title block has no lines to offer.",
+        "LayoutEditor__Snapping__MarkerSizePx": 18,
+        "LayoutEditor__Snapping__ViewportCarryNote": "Viewports move by a point, the way a block moves in CAD. With the Select tool, hover a 2D viewport's linework: the snap marker shows the point a press would carry it by. Drag, and that point snaps to every OTHER viewport's endpoints and midpoints (and the sheet's own vectors and dimension points). Rest the cursor on another drawing's point for AcquireDwellMs - before the drag or during it - and it is acquired as a tracking point (a small cross): the move then locks level with it or plumb below it, with a dashed guide, which is how two elevations line up side by side without being laid over each other. AcquireMax is how many are held at once, oldest dropped first; they are used up by the move they were acquired for, and Escape clears them. Shift holds the move to the nearer axis. ViewportCarry off makes every press move a viewport the plain way; ViewportTracking off keeps the point snap and drops the tracking. Needs Projected Linework on for the viewport being carried, since the points come from it. The snapping toggle switches all of it off.",
+        "LayoutEditor__Snapping__ViewportCarry": true,
+        "LayoutEditor__Snapping__ViewportTracking": true,
+        "LayoutEditor__Snapping__AcquireDwellMs": 400,
+        "LayoutEditor__Snapping__AcquireMax": 3,
+        "LayoutEditor__Snapping__TrackMarkerSizePx": 11
