+    "LayoutEditor__Clipboard__Config": {
+        "LayoutEditor__Clipboard__Description": "Copy, paste and duplicate for viewports, vectors, text, groups, and a multi-selection of vectors or text (Ctrl+C, Ctrl+V, Ctrl+D, and the right-click menu). A paste is a new item with a fresh id: a viewport also gets a new name ('{name} copy', then '{name} copy 2' - wording in the Labels block) and every other setting of the one copied; a vector keeps its vertices, closed, edges, fill, gradient and opacities, offset by PasteOffsetMm. Text, groups and a multi-selection paste the same way, with group members remapped to the new ids. A viewport arrives unlocked. On the sheet it was copied from it steps PasteOffsetMm down and right of the original, and again past any copy already there; on another sheet it lands in the same place; from the menu on bare paper its top-left corner goes at the click. Pointing a viewport copy at a different scene clears the copy name so the caption follows the new scene. CopySnapshot lets a 3D copy show the original's stored picture straight away instead of rendering it again. One paste is one undo step. The clipboard is this tab's memory: it survives changing sheet, not a reload.",
+        "LayoutEditor__Clipboard__PasteOffsetMm": 10,
+        "LayoutEditor__Clipboard__CopySnapshot": true
+    },
+    "LayoutEditor__ModelSource__Config": {
+        "LayoutEditor__ModelSource__Description": "Which design phase a viewport draws - the Model Source row of the Viewport panel, and the Model items on a viewport's right-click menu. The choices are the project's modelGroups (the existing building, a scheme, the next scheme); a viewport that names none draws the Project Default, which is the phase the 3D view opens with: the newest group whose label does not say 'existing'. Everything else stays the viewport's own - scene, cut, window, scale, render composites, model layers, edge styles - so a copy of a viewport switched to another phase is the same view of that model: an existing and a proposed elevation that line up exactly. A phase the 3D view does not hold is loaded off-scene the first time a viewport asks for it (the frame says so while it loads), with the same loader and materials as the 3D view, and goes into the scene only for the length of that viewport's render; the 3D view and the Design Phase menu are never touched. MaxCachedPhases is how many such phases stay loaded at once besides the one in the 3D view; beyond it the least recently drawn is let go and loads again when next wanted. The record key is Viewport__ModelSourceId, holding the group's groupId (its folder name), or null for the Project Default.",
+        "LayoutEditor__ModelSource__MaxCachedPhases": 3
+    },
+    "LayoutEditor__PlanDoors__Config": {
+        "LayoutEditor__PlanDoors__Description": "Doors on 2D viewports: open on plans, shut on elevations and sections. With OpenOnPlans on, a plan draws every door OPEN - in its linework, its base image and the PDF - whatever the 3D view shows, and the model's doors are put straight back afterwards. Each door opens exactly as a click in the 3D view opens it (the ADR / MOD / ROT naming contract): hinged leaves swing, bifolds fold, sliders slide. DrawSwings adds the arc each open hinged leaf's far edge sweeps, at the leaf's floor level and in the Doors layer's line style, one segment per SwingStepDegrees of swing; bifolds and sliders have no arc. With ClickToToggle on, a click on a door in the SELECTED plan viewport closes it and another click opens it again; a press that moves still moves the viewport, and each click waits ClickDelayMs so the two clicks of a double click (entering the content) leave the door alone. The right-click menu offers Close door or Open door for the door under the click, and Open all doors, whatever ClickToToggle says. A lock holds a viewport's frame, not what it draws, so the doors of a locked plan still close and open. Each change is one undo step. The record key is Viewport__ClosedDoors: the door keys drawn shut - an ADR name, or ADR::MOD for one leaf of an exterior double door, whose leaves open one at a time. With ShutOnElevations on, an elevation or section draws every door SHUT - in its linework, its base image and the PDF - whatever the 3D view shows, and has no door to click; off, it draws the doors as the model holds them. 3D viewports always draw the doors as the model holds them.",
+        "LayoutEditor__PlanDoors__OpenOnPlans": true,
+        "LayoutEditor__PlanDoors__ShutOnElevations": true,
+        "LayoutEditor__PlanDoors__DrawSwings": true,
+        "LayoutEditor__PlanDoors__SwingStepDegrees": 5,
+        "LayoutEditor__PlanDoors__ClickToToggle": true,
+        "LayoutEditor__PlanDoors__ClickDelayMs": 300,
+        "LayoutEditor__PlanDoors__HideSwingsNote": "Hide swings, beside Open all on the Viewport panel's Doors row, leaves every door swing off ONE plan viewport - the arcs traced from its open doors AND the SketchUp door swing linework named in SwingCategoryKeys - in its linework, its base image and the PDF. The doors themselves still draw open and still close with a click. It is off by default, except on a plan whose storey is listed in HideSwingsOnStoreys: a roof plan's cut stands above the top storey, so that storey's doors are drawn open and their swings were drawn over the roof. The storey is the plan's own (Dev Tools > Floor Plans > Storey), or the guess from its name and cut height when nobody has picked one. The record key is Viewport__HideSwings, written only once somebody ticks or unticks the box, either way; absent, the storey decides, so an existing roof plan viewport hides its swings without being told and one whose plan stops being a roof plan draws them again.",
+        "LayoutEditor__PlanDoors__HideSwingsOnStoreys": ["roof"],
+        "LayoutEditor__PlanDoors__SwingCategoryKeys": ["ValeVision__Linetype__DoorSwings"]
+    },
+    "LayoutEditor__History__Config": {
+        "LayoutEditor__History__Description": "Undo and redo per sheet. Every announced change is one step (a drag is one step); MaxSteps is how many are kept.",
+        "LayoutEditor__History__MaxSteps": 50
