+    "LayoutEditor__Selection__Config": {
+        "LayoutEditor__Selection__Description": "How a press finds things on the paper. HitToleranceMm is paper millimetres at 100 percent (it shrinks as you zoom in); a drag starts past DragThresholdMm; grips are drawn GripSizePx square on screen at any zoom, and a picked one GripSizePickedPx so the points the next drag will carry read at a glance. A drag that starts where there is nothing to move - bare paper, the grey stage, a locked viewport - draws a selection box once it has travelled BoxStartPx on screen: dragged to the right a window (blue, solid edge) that takes only what it wholly encloses, dragged to the left a crossing (green, dashed edge) that takes anything it touches as well. BoxBorderPx is the box's edge weight on screen. BoxPreview outlines what the box would take while it is dragged, BoxPreviewPadMm clear of each item. The colours are in Na__LayoutEditor__Styles__Main__.css and the modifiers in Na__Hotkeys__DrawingTabs__.json.",
+        "LayoutEditor__Selection__HitToleranceMm": 1.5,
+        "LayoutEditor__Selection__DragThresholdMm": 0.5,
+        "LayoutEditor__Selection__PickDragNote": "A press that PICKS is a pick first. The press that selects something, and the second press of a double click (one landing within DoubleClickSlopPx on screen and DoubleClickMs of the last, on the same item), has to travel PickDragPx on screen before it moves anything - so a pick with a wobble in it never nudges a note, and a fast double click always steps inside rather than shifting what it landed on. It applies where a double click means something: a whole-object move, a viewport's frame or content, a leader, a dimension's value. A press on something already selected moves at the ordinary DragThresholdMm, and vertices, measured points, crop handles and the rotate grip always do. DoubleClickMs should not be shorter than the operating system's own double click time (500 ms on Windows). PickDragPx 0 turns the longer travel off.",
