+    "LayoutEditor__EditScope__Config": {
+        "LayoutEditor__EditScope__Description": "Editing inside a container, the way SketchUp works. Double-click (or press Enter on) a group to step inside it, and a vector to step inside that: while a container is open, only what is inside it answers a press, everything else on the sheet fades to FadeOpacity, and a vector's points are shown and can be dragged, boxed and added to. Click anywhere outside to step back out one level; Escape closes every container, drops the selection and puts the tools down. MoveToolRequired is the other half of the same idea: with it on, a whole object is only moved by the Move tool (M), so a stray drag with Select cannot shift a viewport or a note - grips, crop handles and the points of an open vector are still dragged by Select, because they edit an object rather than relocate it. Turn it off to have Select drag things about as it did before. GrabRadiusPx is how near a point of the open container a press has to land to be taken as a grab at it rather than the start of a selection box - screen pixels, so it is exactly as forgiving as the grip looks at any zoom. GripToleranceFactor widens the reach of a grip inside its own container, where nothing else is competing for the press: missing a corner by a pixel and dragging the whole object instead is what made dimensions feel untouchable.",
+        "LayoutEditor__EditScope__FadeOpacity": 0.45,
+        "LayoutEditor__EditScope__GrabRadiusPx"       : 14,
+        "LayoutEditor__EditScope__GripToleranceFactor": 2,
+        "LayoutEditor__EditScope__MoveToolRequired": true,
+        "LayoutEditor__EditScope__AutoMoveNote": "AutoMoveOnSelect is what stops MoveToolRequired being a chore. Selecting a note, a vector, a leader's bubble or a group is nearly always the first half of moving it, so a Select press on one of the AutoMoveKinds picks the Move tool up by itself and the same press can carry on into the drag. Kinds: annotation (text), shape (vectors), leader (its bubble, note or curve - never its endpoint, which re-points it and stays a grip), group (ordinary and parametric alike), and - left out on purpose - dimension and viewport, which still wait for M so a drawing or a measurement is never shifted by a stray drag. A Move that came up by itself goes back to Select by itself: on a press on anything not listed, when the selection empties, and when a double click steps inside a group, a vector or a text. Press M, or the Move button, for a Move that stays. Several selected items pick Move up only when every one of them is a listed kind. Turn AutoMoveOnSelect off to go back to pressing M every time.",
+        "LayoutEditor__EditScope__AutoMoveOnSelect": true,
+        "LayoutEditor__EditScope__AutoMoveKinds": [ "annotation", "shape", "leader", "group" ]
+    },
+    "LayoutEditor__Lineweights__Config": {
+        "LayoutEditor__Lineweights__Description": "Printed line weights in points (1 pt = 0.3528 mm), the unit a drawing office reads. These are the defaults a new sheet starts with; the Sheet panel edits them per sheet. ViewportPt is the visible projected linework; the hidden, authored and section classes keep their ratios from the Linework block.",
