+        "LayoutEditor__Shapes__AtScaleNote": "Draw at scale - the first control of the Vectors panel, on by default. On, the sizes typed into the Measurements box while the Draw or Rectangle tool is up are real sizes at the drawing's scale: the scale of the 2D viewport under the first point, or the sheet's scale off every viewport (the scale its 2D viewports share, the biggest drawing's when they differ, the Scales default when it has none). 2500 at 1:50 draws 50 mm of paper, and a dimension measuring it at the same scale reads 2500 back. Off, typed sizes are paper millimetres. A setting of the drawing tools, not of a shape: a shape is paper points either way."
+    },
+    "LayoutEditor__Leader__Config": {
+        "LayoutEditor__Leader__Description": "Leaders & Annotation Bubbles: the Leader tool (E) and the Leaders panel. A leader marks a point with an endpoint circle and sweeps to its head on a curve that leaves and arrives level - a straight stub, an S-curve, a straight stub - never a straight rule. Two heads: a Note (multi-line text, one line per Enter) and a Specification bubble (a code centred in a circle, the key the drawing notes will later be pulled in by). The head always extends AWAY from the point, so the side of the point it is placed on decides the handing: to the right a note is left-justified, to the left right-justified, and the leader lands on whichever side of a bubble faces the point. The fill sits behind the text and the line, so a leader masks the drawing it is laid over; FillOpacity lets that show through. Every value here is what a new leader starts with; the Leaders panel edits them, or the selected leader. Weights are printed points, sizes and distances paper millimetres, opacities 0 (clear) to 1 (solid). The records live in each sheet's Sheet__Leaders.",
