+    "LayoutEditor__Measurements__Config": {
+        "LayoutEditor__Measurements__Description": "The Measurements box at the bottom right of the sheet (Na__LayoutEditor__Measurements__), SketchUp's VCB. While the Draw, Rectangle or Dimension tool is up it reads out what is being drawn as it is drawn - a line's length, a rectangle's width x height, a dimension's span and then its line's offset - and a value typed while drawing is used on Enter, with no click into the box first: Draw places the next point that far along the rubber band (its direction, so snaps, Shift and the arrow key axis lock aim it); Rectangle lands the opposite corner (3000 x 2000 or 3000,2000; one figure is a square; typed straight after a rectangle lands, it resizes that rectangle); Dimension picks the end that far along the band, then puts the line that far from what it measures. Dragging a vertex of a finished vector wakes the box the same way: the reading is the drag's length, and a typed value moves the vertex that far along the inferred direction (the original vertex toward where it is being dragged; a minus sign runs the other way) and finishes the drag so the pointer cannot pull it back. Dragging a viewport's frame (not a handle, not the drawing inside) wakes it too: a typed value moves the frame that far along the drag, at the viewport's scale (or the sheet's for a 3D viewport). Moving a vector, a note, a leader, a dimension or a selection whole with the Move tool wakes it the same way, reading how far the move has gone as it goes (along an arrow-key lock, Shift or Ortho too). THE LAST MOVE STAYS IN THE BOX, as in SketchUp: when the button comes up, or a typed value lands the move, the box goes on reading it, and a length typed then lands it again exactly that far along the same line, measured from where it started - 1000, then 1200, then 1150 - until something else is selected, moved or changed, the tool changes or Escape is pressed. A vertex, a dimension end and a dimension line let go by the mouse stay typeable the same way. A typed value is absolute: no snap, grid, Shift or Ortho moves it. A number with no unit is millimetres; add m, cm or mm for another (2.5m), commas may group thousands (2,500) and a minus sign draws the other way. The line above the box shows how a value is being read as it is typed. AT SCALE: with Draw at scale on (Shapes DefaultAtScale) lines, rectangles and a dragged vertex take and show real sizes at the drawing's scale - the 2D viewport under the first point (or the vertex), or the sheet's scale elsewhere - so 2500 at 1:50 is 50 mm of paper; off, they are paper millimetres. A dimension uses Measure at scale (Dimensions DefaultAtScale). The chip at the right of the box shows the scale in use. Precision is the decimal places a reading shows (trailing zeros dropped), UnitsSuffix follows it, PairSeparator joins a rectangle's two sizes, HintMs is how long a message above the box stays, and EdgeGapPx keeps the box clear of the stage's scrollbars. The keys it reads are in Na__Hotkeys__DrawingTabs__.json under MeasurementsBox.",
